// expect: answers total = 42
// why: an async method builds a machine like a free function, and `this` survives the suspension
// spec: §wac-async-method-4kx7vqd

/**
 * `async` on a **method**, lowered and run.
 *
 * The receiver is an ordinary parameter, so the machine that already carries arguments across a
 * suspension carries `this` too — which is why the lowering needed no new mechanism, only somewhere
 * to look. `lowerProgram` matched `case Func(…)` and let every other declaration fall into an
 * `else`, so a struct's methods were never offered to it, and the four registries that size the
 * synthesised machine could not see them either.
 *
 * `total` awaits **twice**, so the machine has two suspension points and has to come back to the
 * same receiver both times: if `this` were not carried across, `base` would not read 20 on the
 * second resumption and the answer would not be 42.
 *
 * **This case was `expect: declined` from 2026-08-31 to 2026-09-01**, and before that the same
 * source was a parse error saying *expected a type … found 'async'* — a diagnostic about where the
 * parser had got to, whose help line offered to accept a type, which is advice that cannot be
 * followed. The three front-end fixes landed first and deliberately without the lowering, so the
 * refusal named the real thing while the work was outstanding. `issues/lang/0301b`.
 *
 * **It suspends on a hand-built ticket, and that is a correction rather than a simplification.**
 * The case asked for `Cli.readFile` until it was run under the corpus, which drives a case with no
 * capabilities: it answered nothing at all rather than a number, and a grant failure is not a test
 * failure. `Pending<i32>.driven` is how `0306` makes a ticket for the same reason.
 *
 * **Two shapes it deliberately does not use, because neither works for a free function either.**
 * `await` on a call to another `async` function is `a call to Pending`, and on a call to another
 * `async` method is `a null in a Pending<i32> slot` — both reproduce with no method in sight, so
 * they are limits of the lowering as a whole rather than anything about a receiver. Checked, not
 * assumed: the free-function control fails identically. `issues/lang/0301b`.
 */
import { Pending } from "std/platform.wac";

/**
 * A ticket that is already answered, built by hand — the same helper `0306` uses.
 *
 * A case is driven with no capabilities, so a suspension has to be on a ticket the program makes
 * rather than one a `Cli` hands back.
 */
Pending<i32> ready(i32 v) {
  i32[] cell = i32[1](fill: v);
  fn[i32(i32)] r = (i32 id) => cell[0];
  fn[bool(i32)] s = (i32 id) => true;
  fn[void(i32)] d = (i32 id) => { };
  return Pending<i32>.driven(0, r, s, d);
}

struct Counter {
  i32 base;

  /** Two suspensions, and `this.base` read again after the second. */
  async i32 both(this) {
    i32 a = await ready(this.base);
    i32 b = await ready(this.base + 2);
    return a + b;
  }
}

export i32 total() {
  Counter c = Counter(20);
  return c.both().wait();
}
